When I build an application, one decision always shows up sooner or later: how I am going to
handle its state.

The first things I think about are how much it will grow, what information its screens will
share and what I would gain by centralising it. They are useful questions, but over time I have
learned they are not enough.

An application can have many screens and little coordination between them. Another can have a
single screen and still be juggling a form, an autosave, server responses and changes nobody has
confirmed yet.

So what should we look at? Managing state means deciding where information lives, who can change
it and how long it should exist. The library comes after.

## What state is

State is the information the application needs to remember to know what to show and how to
respond at a given moment.

Take a bookings table. Its state can include:

- the bookings received from the server,
- the filters you applied,
- the page you are on,
- the selected rows,
- whether a request is in flight,
- the changes you typed but have not saved.

State exists even without a library: a boolean that opens a modal is state too. And not
everything needs storing. If the number of selected rows can be computed from the selection, you
usually do not need to keep both.

## When several parts must agree

You edit a booking from a dialog. On save, the table row must update, maybe the detail too, and a
counter. If each part keeps its own copy, someone has to keep the copies in sync.

{% diagram src="where-state-lives/copies.en.svg" alt="Left: an edit dialog saves status confirmed but updates only the booking row; the detail still shows pending and the counter still shows 3. Right: the dialog writes one source, booking 42, and the row, the detail and the counter all read confirmed and 2 from it." caption="With copies, every save has to reach every copy. With one source, every part reads the same value." /%}

That is where the familiar problems come from:

- a screen shows old information,
- two components send the same request,
- a late response replaces a newer result,
- you switch to another record and the previous draft is still there,
- you cannot tell which operation started a loading indicator.

This is the problem a store can solve. Not the only way to solve it, though.

## What organising state buys, and what it costs

The benefits come from organising state well, not from a particular tool. You can have a global
store and still have badly designed state.

| What we can gain | What we have to take on |
| --- | --- |
| A shared source of reference | Deciding who is responsible for each piece of data |
| More predictable changes | Rules and conventions for updates |
| Easier tracking of events | More structure, and more to learn |
| Less manual syncing between views | Policies for refreshing and invalidating |
| Logic that is easier to test on its own | Keeping the store from collecting everything |

Centralising can simplify a lot, but it can also turn a local problem into a dependency of the
whole application. That is not an argument against stores: they have a cost, and we need to know
what we are buying with it.

## Not all state belongs in one place

Ask a few questions about each piece of state:

| Question | Example | Where I would put it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Does it only matter to this component? | An open dropdown | Local state |
| Do I want to share it in a link? | Filters and pagination | The URL |
| Is it remote data that can go stale? | Search results | A query layer or cache |
| Does it belong to one process? | The draft of a wizard | Feature state |
| Do different areas of the app need it? | The active organisation | Shared app state |
| Can it be computed from other values? | The selected total | Derived state |

{% diagram src="where-state-lives/places.en.svg" alt="The bookings screen with each piece outlined by where it lives: status and page in the URL, the rows in a server cache, the open row menu in the component, the edit drawer with its unsaved draft in feature state, the active organisation Acme in app-wide state, and 2 selected as derived state." caption="One screen, six places. The legend maps each colour to where that piece of state lives." /%}

One distinction is especially useful: the saved booking and the draft of its edit. They are
related, but they have different owners and different lifetimes. The saved booking is server data
that other screens read and that can go stale. The draft belongs to the edit: it starts when you
open the dialog, holds changes nobody has confirmed, and should disappear when you save or cancel.
Keep them in the same object and closing the dialog has to undo half of it, or the next record
opens with the previous draft.

## When you need more structure

I would not use a formula like «more than five components means NgRx». These are the signals that
make me reconsider:

- every change requires telling several components by hand,
- the update rules are spread across many screens,
- there are concurrent operations and responses that can arrive out of order,
- you need to undo, retry or roll back optimistic changes,
- the team needs a shared way of working,
- it is hard to reconstruct why the application reached a given state.

I worry less about how many components read a piece of data and more about how many can change
it, under which rules and with what consequences.

## Choosing the tool

By now the comparison has criteria. I would start from the need:

- **Simple local state:** the framework's own tools.
- **A feature with its own logic:** a service or a scoped store.
- **Remote data:** something that handles queries, caching and invalidation, such as TanStack
  Query.
- **Many events and coordinated effects:** a more explicit architecture of actions, transitions
  and effects.

NgRx Store fits the last case in Angular, with actions, reducers and effects; Redux Toolkit plays a
similar role in React. NgRx SignalStore fits a feature with its own logic, and can also be shared
across the app. Zustand is a small store for React without actions. They are not interchangeable,
and they are not a ranking of small, medium and large: some belong to different frameworks.

The team matters too: familiarity, existing conventions and the cost of maintaining one more
technology. A tool is not chosen by how comfortable the first component feels.

## The same form, with and without {% highlight %}SignalStore{% /highlight %}

A form to create customers: name and email, a call to an API, and the saving state, the error and
the customers created in this session. Both versions manage state; the difference is where it
lives. The `FormGroup` itself already handles values, validation and changes.

They share the API service, which makes the request but stores no customers, and a function that
builds the form:

```ts
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class CustomersApi {
  private readonly http = inject(HttpClient);

  create(input: CreateCustomer) {
    return this.http.post<Customer>('/api/customers', input);
  }
}

export function createCustomerForm() {
  return new FormGroup({
    name: new FormControl('', {
      nonNullable: true,
      validators: [Validators.required],
    }),
    email: new FormControl('', {
      nonNullable: true,
      validators: [Validators.required, Validators.email],
    }),
  });
}
```

**Without a store**, the component holds the form, calls the service and updates its own signals:

```ts
export class CustomerFormLocalComponent {
  private readonly api = inject(CustomersApi);
  private readonly destroyRef = inject(DestroyRef);

  readonly form = createCustomerForm();
  readonly customers = signal<Customer[]>([]);
  readonly saving = signal(false);
  readonly error = signal<string | null>(null);

  submit() {
    if (this.saving()) return;
    if (this.form.invalid) {
      this.form.markAllAsTouched();
      return;
    }

    this.saving.set(true);
    this.error.set(null);

    this.api
      .create(this.form.getRawValue())
      .pipe(
        takeUntilDestroyed(this.destroyRef),
        finalize(() => this.saving.set(false)),
      )
      .subscribe({
        next: (customer) =>
          this.customers.update((current) => [...current, customer]),
        error: () =>
          this.error.set('Could not create the customer. Try again.'),
      });
  }
}
```

If everything belongs to this screen, this is a perfectly reasonable solution. There is nothing
wrong with three signals and an HTTP call in a small component.

**With SignalStore**, the customers, the saving state, the error and the logic that updates them
move to the store. The form stays in the component: its controls and validation do not need to
become shared state.

```ts
export const CustomersStore = signalStore(
  withState<CustomersState>({
    customers: [],
    saving: false,
    error: null,
  }),
  withMethods((store, api = inject(CustomersApi)) => ({
    create: rxMethod<CreateCustomer>(
      pipe(
        exhaustMap((input) => {
          patchState(store, { saving: true, error: null });
          return api.create(input).pipe(
            tapResponse({
              next: (customer) =>
                patchState(store, (state) => ({
                  customers: [...state.customers, customer],
                })),
              error: () =>
                patchState(store, {
                  error: 'Could not create the customer. Try again.',
                }),
              finalize: () => patchState(store, { saving: false }),
            }),
          );
        }),
      ),
    ),
  })),
);
```

`exhaustMap` keeps a second creation from starting while one is still running. The error is
handled inside that operation, so you can try again after a failure. The component now reads the
store's signals and calls one method:

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-customer-form-store',
  imports: [ReactiveFormsModule],
  providers: [CustomersStore],
  templateUrl: './customer-form.html',
})
export class CustomerFormStoreComponent {
  readonly store = inject(CustomersStore);
  readonly form = createCustomerForm();

  // References to the store's signals, not copies of its state.
  readonly customers = this.store.customers;
  readonly saving = this.store.saving;
  readonly error = this.store.error;

  submit() {
    if (this.saving()) return;
    if (this.form.invalid) {
      this.form.markAllAsTouched();
      return;
    }
    this.store.create(this.form.getRawValue());
  }
}
```

The template is exactly the same. What changed is who is responsible for the operation and the
state it produces.

| | State in the component | State in the store |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Form values and validation | Component | Component |
| Saving, error and result | Component | Store |
| The HTTP call | Coordinated by the component | Coordinated by the store |
| Testing the operation | With the component, or by extracting logic | Apart from the presentation |
| Sharing between components | Needs inputs, outputs or a service | They can use one shared instance |
| Initial cost | Lower | More structure and dependencies |

For this isolated form I would start with the first version. I would consider the second when
creating a customer becomes part of a feature with more consumers, rules or transitions. The
advantage of the store is not that state disappears or that there is less code in total: its
responsibilities and rules end up in an explicit place.

## A store still needs a place

Choosing a store does not decide where it lives. Three separate questions:

- **Scope:** who needs access?
- **Lifetime:** when should it reset?
- **Loading:** when does its code join the application?

In Angular, `providers: [CustomersStore]` makes that store local to the component that provides
it: an instance is created with the component and goes away with it. It is not global. If you
later split the form and the list into sibling components, provide the store in their container:

```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-customers-page',
  imports: [CustomerCreateComponent, CustomerListComponent],
  providers: [CustomersStore],
  template: `
    <app-customer-create />
    <app-customer-list />
  `,
})
export class CustomersPageComponent {}
```

Both children `inject(CustomersStore)` and get the container's instance, as long as they do not
provide it again themselves. A feature registered in NgRx's global store is different: it joins
the one store the whole application shares, and its state stays there after you leave the page.

The clearest case: if I open two editors at once, do I want them to share the same draft, or each
to have its own?

{% diagram src="where-state-lives/scope.en.svg" alt="Left: customers-page provides CustomersStore, and its children customer-create and customer-list both inject instance number 1. Right: bookings-page has two booking-editor children, each providing its own DraftStore, so they get instances 1 and 2." caption="Where you provide the store decides who shares an instance." /%}

## Where I start now

The size of the application is still something I take into account, but I try not to start
there.

First I look at what information I need to keep, who can change it, which parts depend on it and
when it should stop existing. With those answers, choosing a tool gets much easier. Sometimes the
answer is a store. Other times it is keeping that state in the component, moving it to the URL,
or letting a cache handle the server's data.

What I am after is that every change has a clear place, and that understanding the application
does not depend on remembering how five screens keep each other in sync.

{% quiz %}
{% question text="The bookings table's filters and page should still be there when you share the link with a colleague. Where do they live?" options=["Local state", "The URL", "Feature state", "Shared app state"] answer=2 explanation="If you want to share it in a link, the note puts it in the URL, like filters and pagination." /%}

{% question text="You can compute how many rows are selected from the selection itself. What does the note suggest?" options=["Store the count next to the selection", "Derive it: no need to keep both", "Move both to a global store"] answer=2 explanation="Not everything needs storing: a value computed from other values is derived state." /%}

{% question text="Deciding whether a piece of data needs more structure, what does the note weigh most?" options=["How many components read it", "How many can change it, under which rules and with what consequences", "How many screens the application has"] answer=2 explanation="The note worries less about how many components read a piece of data and more about how many can change it, under which rules and with what consequences." /%}

{% question text="You move the customer creation from the component to a SignalStore. What is the real advantage?" options=["There is less code in total", "The form no longer has state", "Its responsibilities and rules end up in an explicit place"] answer=3 explanation="State does not disappear and there is not less code: the operation and its state get an explicit owner. The form stays in the component." /%}

{% question text="You open two booking editors at once. How many `DraftStore` instances are there?" options=["One, shared by both editors", "Two: each editor provides its own", "One for the whole application"] answer=2 explanation="`providers: [DraftStore]` on the editor creates an instance with each editor, so each one keeps its own draft." %}
```ts
@Component({
  selector: 'app-booking-editor',
  providers: [DraftStore],
  templateUrl: './booking-editor.html',
})
export class BookingEditorComponent {
  readonly draft = inject(DraftStore);
}
```
{% /question %}
